<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Liang (Liang Shidu)</h1><p class="st-meta">617 CE – 628 CE · capital Shuofang</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A rebel state on the Ordos frontier founded during the collapse of Sui, which survived as a client of the Eastern Turks until 628, the last regime to resist the Tang.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liang-shidu">Liang Shidu</a>, a local officer in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shuofang-commandery">Shuofang</a> commandery on the northern bend of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a>, killed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a> governor in 617 and proclaimed himself emperor of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/liang-li-gui">Liang</a>. To survive he accepted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> status and a title from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/eastern-turkic-khaganate">Eastern Turkic Khaganate</a>, whose cavalry protected him.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Little is recorded of its administration. It held a thinly populated frontier zone of steppe and farmland and served the Turks as a base for raids into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-tang">Tang</a> heartland. Its dependence on the khaganate meant its fortunes rose and fell with Turkish power.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>When the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/eastern-turkic-khaganate">Eastern Turkic Khaganate</a> weakened in the late 620s, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-tang">Tang</a> armies besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shuofang-commandery">Shuofang</a> in 628. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liang-shidu">Liang Shidu</a> was killed by his cousin <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/liang-li-gui">Liang</a> Luoren, who surrendered the city, completing the Tang reunification of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> two years before the Turkish khaganate itself fell.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 617 CE – 628 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><cir